window.CH16_LESSON_CARDS=[{"id":"ch16-lesson-001","term":"Eustress vs. distress","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress basics","cue":"Think: helpful stress vs. harmful stress","simple":"Stress can have positive effects when it motivates or energizes a person (eustress) and negative effects when demands overwhelm coping or harm well-being (distress).","examples":["Planning a wedding can be stressful but positive and motivating.","Job loss or eviction can produce harmful distress."],"apply":["Getting engaged and planning a wedding creates stress but is experienced positively. Which type of stress does the chapter use this to illustrate?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-002","term":"Stressor vs. stress","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress basics","cue":"Think: stimulus vs. response","simple":"A stressor is the source or stimulus; stress is the person's response to that stressor.","examples":["An upcoming exam is a stressor; the emotional and physiological reaction to it is stress."],"apply":["An upcoming exam triggers tension, worry, and physiological arousal. Identify the stressor and the stress response."]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-003","term":"Brief vs. chronic stressors","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress basics","cue":"Think: short-term vs. prolonged","simple":"Brief stressors are short-term challenges, while chronic stressors are prolonged and ongoing.","examples":["A traffic jam is a brief stressor.","Ongoing legal problems, job insecurity, or prolonged illness can be chronic stressors."],"apply":["Which is more likely to be chronic: a traffic jam or two years of financial problems? Explain why."]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-004","term":"Sympathetic stress response","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress physiology","cue":"Think: fight or flight","simple":"The sympathetic division of the autonomic nervous system prepares the body to respond to danger by increasing heart rate, blood pressure, and respiration while suppressing less immediately essential processes such as digestion.","examples":["A perceived threat produces a racing heart and faster breathing.","The chapter quiz asks what the sympathetic division does and identifies preparing the body to react to danger."],"apply":["A threat causes rapid heart rate and breathing while digestion slows. Which branch of the autonomic nervous system is driving this response?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-005","term":"GAS sequence","type":"lesson-concept","category":"General adaptation syndrome","cue":"Think: alarm → resistance → exhaustion","simple":"Selye's general adaptation syndrome progresses from alarm to resistance to exhaustion as a stressor persists.","examples":["Alarm mobilizes resources; resistance reflects ongoing coping; exhaustion occurs after resources are depleted."],"apply":["Put the three GAS stages in order from first to last."]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-006","term":"Amygdala threat detection","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress physiology","cue":"Think: detects threat","simple":"The amygdala rapidly identifies potentially threatening stimuli and helps initiate the stress response.","examples":["After a stimulus is judged potentially dangerous, the SAM system and HPA axis are mobilized."],"apply":["What is the amygdala's main role at the beginning of the stress response in this chapter?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-007","term":"SAM vs. HPA response","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress physiology","cue":"Think: fast catecholamines vs. slower cortisol","simple":"The SAM system responds quickly with adrenaline/epinephrine and norepinephrine for immediate fight-or-flight effects, while the HPA axis leads to cortisol release for a longer stress response.","examples":["SAM activity raises heart rate and respiration quickly.","HPA activation results in cortisol release from the adrenal cortex."],"apply":["A hormone response is immediate and short-lived versus slower and cortisol-based. Which system fits each response?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-008","term":"Early-life severe stress effects","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress and health","cue":"Think: stress can alter later reactivity","simple":"The chapter describes severe early-life stress as being associated with heightened amygdala responses, norepinephrine-system hyperactivity, increased HPA responses, reduced hippocampal neurogenesis, and greater later vulnerability to stress.","examples":["The quiz also uses misalignment of the front teeth as an indicator associated with significant stress exposure early in life."],"apply":["Why can severe stress early in life make a person more vulnerable to stress later, according to the lesson?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-009","term":"Acute vs. chronic stress effects","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress and health","cue":"Think: short-term can help; long-term can harm","simple":"Short-term stress can temporarily increase alertness and energy, while chronic activation of the stress response can suppress body systems and harm physical and mental health.","examples":["Chronic stress is linked in the lesson with obesity, heart disease, reproductive problems, and skin conditions."],"apply":["Why can short-term stress sometimes be useful while chronic stress is more likely to damage health?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-010","term":"Cortisol and immune suppression","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress and health","cue":"Think: prolonged stress suppresses immunity","simple":"Cortisol released during prolonged stress can suppress immune functioning; chronic stress can reduce lymphocyte activity and impair healing or resistance to illness.","examples":["The quiz identifies cortisol as the hormone that suppresses the immune system during prolonged stress."],"apply":["Which stress hormone should you connect with prolonged stress and immune suppression?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-011","term":"Type A hostility and health risk","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Personality and stress","cue":"Think: hostility is the key risk","simple":"The chapter emphasizes that the heart-disease risk associated with Type A personality is driven especially by hostility rather than competitiveness alone.","examples":["Hostile people may experience more conflict, less social support, and higher stress-related mortality."],"apply":["A Type A person is competitive and hardworking but not hostile. Which trait does the chapter identify as the stronger heart-disease risk factor?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-012","term":"Choosing a coping strategy","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Coping with stress","cue":"Think: control matters","simple":"Problem-focused coping is most useful when the stressor can be changed; emotion-focused coping is useful when the stressor is outside one's control; relationship-focused coping aims to protect social relationships.","examples":["Changing a study schedule for an exam is problem-focused.","Managing sadness after an uncontrollable event is emotion-focused."],"apply":["A stressor cannot be changed, but the person's negative feelings can be managed. Which coping approach fits best?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-013","term":"Effective stress management","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Coping with stress","cue":"Think: control + exercise + support + meditation","simple":"The chapter recommends reducing or eliminating stressors where possible, building an internal sense of control, exercising, using social support, and practicing meditation rather than withdrawing or avoiding support.","examples":["Keeping problems entirely to yourself is not presented as an effective strategy.","Regular aerobic exercise, social support, and meditation are presented as useful stress-management tools."],"apply":["Which combination better reflects the chapter's stress-management advice: isolation and avoidance, or control, exercise, connection, and meditation?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-014","term":"Psychological hardiness","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Coping with stress","cue":"Think: commitment + control + challenge","simple":"Hardiness is a resilience-related personality pattern made up of commitment, control, and challenge.","examples":["Commitment means involvement rather than withdrawal.","Control means believing you can influence events.","Challenge means viewing change as an opportunity for learning and growth."],"apply":["What three components make up psychological hardiness?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-015","term":"Health as complete well-being","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Psychology and health","cue":"Think: physical + mental + social","simple":"The chapter quiz uses the World Health Organization definition of health as a state of complete physical, mental, and social well-being.","examples":["Modern health psychology treats health as broader than the absence of disease."],"apply":["According to the chapter's WHO item, what three domains are included in health?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-016","term":"Biopsychosocial health example","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Psychology and health","cue":"Think: genes + behavior/stress + social support","simple":"The biopsychosocial approach explains health outcomes by combining biological factors, psychological factors, and social factors.","examples":["A weight-related health case can include family history, personal stress, and friends' support for eating habits."],"apply":["A health explanation considers family history, personal stress, and social support together. Which model is being applied?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-017","term":"Health behaviors and preventable risk","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Psychology and health","cue":"Think: smoking is the leading preventable cause","simple":"The chapter identifies smoking as the leading preventable cause of death in the United States and highlights exercise, healthy weight, and a balanced diet as important health behaviors.","examples":["The lesson says four habits—no smoking, about 30 minutes of daily exercise, healthy weight, and a fruit/vegetable-rich diet—can greatly reduce chronic-disease risk."],"apply":["Which behavior does the chapter identify as the leading preventable cause of death in the United States?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-018","term":"Health disparities and poverty","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Psychology and health","cue":"Think: access and socioeconomic status matter","simple":"The chapter notes disparities in access to mental-health care and states that poverty is an important risk factor for psychological disorders.","examples":["Underrepresented groups may have less access to services, receive poorer-quality care, and be underrepresented in research.","People at the lowest socioeconomic levels are described as experiencing psychological disorders at higher rates."],"apply":["How can social and economic conditions influence health according to this lesson?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-019","term":"Positive psychology focus","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Positive psychology","cue":"Think: strengths, growth, fulfillment","simple":"Positive psychology focuses on normal behavior, human strengths, growth, fulfillment, and happiness while using scientific reasoning and methods.","examples":["The quiz contrasts general psychology's broad study of behavior, mental processes, and brain function with positive psychology's emphasis on human growth and fulfillment."],"apply":["What distinguishes the focus of positive psychology from the broader focus of general psychology?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-020","term":"PERMA model","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Positive psychology","cue":"Think: positive emotion, engagement, relationships, meaning, accomplishment","simple":"Seligman's PERMA model describes five elements of well-being: positive emotion, engagement, relationships, meaning, and accomplishment.","examples":["A person can evaluate well-being by considering all five PERMA domains rather than happiness alone."],"apply":["Name the five elements in the PERMA model."]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-021","term":"Hedonic vs. eudaimonic well-being","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Positive psychology","cue":"Think: pleasure vs. meaning/self-realization","simple":"A hedonic approach emphasizes obtaining pleasure and avoiding pain, while a eudaimonic approach emphasizes meaningfulness and self-realization.","examples":["Pleasure-seeking fits the hedonic perspective.","Living according to deeply meaningful goals fits the eudaimonic perspective."],"apply":["A person values meaning and self-realization more than immediate pleasure. Which perspective fits?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-022","term":"Traits that support well-being","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Positive psychology","cue":"Think: hope, resilience, spirituality, gratitude","simple":"The chapter identifies hope, resilience, spirituality, and gratitude as traits that contribute to well-being.","examples":["A gratitude journal is used as an example of a practice that can contribute to well-being.","Hope is expecting goals to be met in the future."],"apply":["Which four traits does the chapter explicitly connect with well-being?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-023","term":"Positive institutions","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Positive psychology","cue":"Think: shared purpose + fair rules + reciprocal care","simple":"Positive institutions such as families, schools, and community centers support well-being through shared goals, fair rules and consequences, and reciprocal caring.","examples":["A school with a clear shared purpose, fair expectations, and mutual care illustrates a positive institution."],"apply":["What features make an institution 'positive' in the chapter's discussion?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-024","term":"Wealth and happiness","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Positive psychology","cue":"Think: more money is not unlimited happiness","simple":"The chapter quiz states that once basic needs are met, additional money does not guarantee greater happiness.","examples":["Personal relationships are emphasized as an important contributor to happiness."],"apply":["After a person's basic needs are met, does the chapter say that additional income automatically guarantees greater happiness?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-025","term":"Loneliness and health","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Psychology and health","cue":"Think: social isolation affects more than social well-being","simple":"Loneliness is linked with multiple health outcomes, including poorer sleep, greater stress, depression risk, and broader physical and mental health effects.","examples":["The quiz marks false the claim that loneliness matters only for social well-being."],"apply":["Why is it incorrect to say loneliness affects only social well-being?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-026","term":"Stress-and-inflammation study variables","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress and immune functioning","cue":"Think: CRP and IL-6 were outcomes","simple":"In the Oshri et al. study summarized in the chapter, C-reactive protein and interleukin-6 were biomarkers of systemic inflammation and served as dependent variables.","examples":["The study examined family stress, autonomic nervous system reactivity, and inflammatory biomarkers in children ages 9-12."],"apply":["In the chapter's research study, were C-reactive protein and interleukin-6 independent variables or dependent variables?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-027","term":"Autonomic nervous system moderates stress and inflammation","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress and immune functioning","cue":"Think: body regulation changes the effect","simple":"The research activity found that family stress was not directly linked to higher inflammation in every child; autonomic nervous system reactivity helped determine whether family stress was associated with inflammation.","examples":["The sympathetic branch mobilizes energy for immediate demands, while the parasympathetic branch supports recovery and balance.","The activity notes that mindfulness training may help alter autonomic regulation."],"apply":["According to the research activity, which body system moderated the relationship between family stress and inflammation?"]},{"id":"ch16-lesson-028","term":"Positive outcomes of stress","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress basics","cue":"stress can help","simple":"Stress is not always harmful; it can motivate performance and improve fitness.","examples":["The lesson says racing in a 5K produces stress but enhances fitness and health.","The chapter notes that stress can motivate a person to perform well."],"apply":["Racing in a 5K is stressful, yet the lesson calls it beneficial. What positive outcome does it produce?"],"compare":"Eustress is positive stress itself; positive outcomes are the good results stress can produce, like better fitness."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-029","term":"Perception of danger initiates the stress response","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress basics","cue":"danger seen, stress starts","simple":"Stress begins when you perceive something as dangerous.","examples":["The lesson defines stress as an unpleasant emotional state resulting from the perception of danger.","The quiz feedback notes that a stimulus interpreted as dangerous may initiate the alarm reaction."],"apply":["Two students face the same pop quiz, but only one feels stressed. What explains the difference?"],"compare":"A stressor is the stimulus; the perception of danger is what turns it into a stress response."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-030","term":"Stress response evolved to enhance survival","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress basics","cue":"stress is a survival tool","simple":"The stress response mobilizes the body's resources to help you survive.","examples":["The lesson says the stress response includes physiological and behavioral aspects that mobilize the body's resources.","The chapter frames this mobilization as enhancing one's survival."],"apply":["Why would the body shut down digestion when facing a sudden threat?"],"compare":"Fight-or-flight is the immediate reaction; survival mobilization is its evolutionary purpose."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-031","term":"Coordinated physical, cognitive, and behavioral stress reaction","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress basics","cue":"body, mind, behavior together","simple":"Stress triggers a coordinated reaction of physical, cognitive, and behavioral responses.","examples":["The lesson states the stress response elicits physical, cognitive, and behavioral responses to cope with the stressor.","The chapter quiz asks which nervous system division prepares the body to react to perceived danger."],"apply":["Racing heart, worried thoughts, and pacing during finals week show which feature of the stress response?"],"compare":"A stressor is the trigger; this coordinated reaction is the body's full response to it."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-032","term":"Nonessential processes inhibited during fight-or-flight","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress physiology","cue":"digestion pauses in danger","simple":"During fight-or-flight, the body inhibits processes like digestion that are nonessential for immediate survival.","examples":["The lesson says heart rate, blood pressure, and respiration increase while digestion is inhibited.","The chapter notes the body diverts resources away from nonessential processes."],"apply":["Your stomach feels unsettled right before a big presentation. Which fight-or-flight change explains it?"],"compare":"Essential processes speed up, like the heart and lungs; nonessential ones like digestion slow down."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-033","term":"Adrenal medulla vs adrenal cortex","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress physiology","cue":"medulla fast, cortex slow","simple":"The adrenal medulla releases adrenaline and norepinephrine, while the adrenal cortex releases cortisol.","examples":["The lesson says the SAM system releases adrenaline and norepinephrine from the adrenal medulla.","The chapter says HPA axis activation releases cortisol from the adrenal cortex."],"apply":["A question asks which adrenal region releases cortisol during prolonged stress. What is the answer?"],"compare":"Medulla: quick adrenaline burst. Cortex: slower cortisol release."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-034","term":"Cortisol increases available energy","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress physiology","cue":"cortisol fuels the body","simple":"Cortisol increases the energy available to the body during stress.","examples":["The lesson lists increasing available energy among cortisol's effects.","The chapter pairs this with cortisol suppressing the immune system."],"apply":["During a stressful week, your body stays fueled even with little rest. Which cortisol effect helps?"],"compare":"Adrenaline gives an immediate burst; cortisol sustains energy over longer stress."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-035","term":"Reduced hippocampal neurogenesis from early-life stress","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress physiology","cue":"early stress slows brain growth","simple":"Severe stress early in life reduces the growth of new neurons in the hippocampus.","examples":["The lesson lists reduced hippocampal neurogenesis among the lasting effects of severe early-life stress.","The chapter links these changes to greater vulnerability to stress later in life."],"apply":["A child who endured severe early stress shows memory difficulties as an adult. Which brain change from the lesson fits?"],"compare":"Amygdala changes heighten threat reactions; hippocampal changes impair learning and memory."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-036","term":"Mental clarity during the alarm reaction","type":"lesson-concept","category":"General adaptation syndrome","cue":"alarm brings sharp focus","simple":"The alarm reaction brings sympathetic arousal plus mental clarity.","examples":["The quiz describes the alarm reaction as characterized by sympathetic arousal and mental clarity.","The lesson says all bodily resources are mobilized during the alarm stage."],"apply":["In the first moments of an emergency, people often report unusually sharp thinking. Which GAS stage is this?"],"compare":"Alarm: mobilization and clarity. Exhaustion: depletion and breakdown."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-037","term":"Selye expanded Cannon's fight-or-flight work","type":"lesson-concept","category":"General adaptation syndrome","cue":"Selye built on Cannon","simple":"Selye's general adaptation syndrome expanded on Cannon's earlier work on fight-or-flight.","examples":["The quiz feedback states that Selye expanded on Cannon's work on stress.","The chapter notes Selye's alarm reaction corresponds with Cannon's fight-or-flight state."],"apply":["A test asks whose work Selye's general adaptation syndrome built upon. What is the answer?"],"compare":"Cannon described fight-or-flight; Selye added the three-stage pattern of alarm, resistance, and exhaustion."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-038","term":"Immune system is least essential during the stress response","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress and health","cue":"immunity waits its turn","simple":"During an immediate stress response, the immune system is the least essential system.","examples":["The quiz asks which body system is least essential when responding to stress, and the answer is the immune system.","The lesson explains that resources shift toward immediate survival."],"apply":["If the body must choose during a sudden threat, which system gets the lowest priority?"],"compare":"The cardiovascular system is critical right now; the immune system can wait until the threat passes."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-039","term":"Lymphocytes suppressed, not red blood cells","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress and immune functioning","cue":"lymphocytes down, RBCs fine","simple":"Chronic stress suppresses lymphocytes, the immune cells, but not red blood cells.","examples":["The quiz asks about chronic stress effects on the immune system, and the answer is suppression of lymphocytes.","The chapter distinguishes this from suppression of erythrocytes, which is incorrect."],"apply":["A patient under chronic stress shows low lymphocyte counts but normal red blood cells. Which quiz fact does this match?"],"compare":"Lymphocytes fight infection and drop under chronic stress; erythrocytes carry oxygen and are unaffected."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-040","term":"Chronic stress health problems","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress and health","cue":"chronic stress harms the body","simple":"Chronic stress can contribute to obesity, heart disease, reproductive problems, and acne.","examples":["The lesson lists obesity, heart disease, reproductive problems, and skin conditions such as acne.","The chapter contrasts these with the beneficial effects of short-term stress."],"apply":["A student under months of stress develops skin breakouts and weight gain. Which lesson concept fits?"],"compare":"Acute stress is brief and can help; chronic stress is prolonged and damages health."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-041","term":"Stress, cortisol, and poor sleep","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress and health","cue":"cortisol ruins sleep","simple":"Excessive cortisol from stress decreases both the quality and quantity of sleep.","examples":["The quiz states that stress decreases sleep quality and quantity due to excessive cortisol.","The chapter links prolonged stress activation to disrupted bodily systems."],"apply":["During finals week, a student sleeps fewer hours and wakes up tired. Which hormone does the lesson blame?"],"compare":"Acute stress can energize you; chronic cortisol elevation wears sleep down."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-042","term":"Stress can increase empathy","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Stress basics","cue":"stress builds empathy","simple":"Experiencing stress can increase a person's ability to empathize with others.","examples":["The lesson lists increased empathy as a positive outcome of stress.","The chapter pairs this with stress motivating people to perform well."],"apply":["After a tough semester, a student finds herself more understanding of classmates' struggles. Which positive outcome of stress is this?"],"compare":"Distress harms well-being; increased empathy is one of stress's potential benefits."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-043","term":"Sense of control affects the stress response","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Coping with stress","cue":"control calms stress","simple":"How much control you feel you have considerably affects your stress response.","examples":["The lesson says our response to stress is considerably affected by the sense of control we have.","The quiz lists establishing an internal sense of control as a powerful stress management technique."],"apply":["Two employees face the same deadline, but the one who chose her own schedule feels calmer. What explains this?"],"compare":"The stressor may be uncontrollable; your sense of control over your response still matters."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-044","term":"Aerobic exercise as stress management","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Coping with stress","cue":"exercise burns off stress","simple":"Regular aerobic exercise is an effective way to manage stress.","examples":["The lesson names regular aerobic exercise alongside social support and meditation as effective stress management.","The quiz feedback lists exercise as a technique for managing emotions triggered by a stressor."],"apply":["A student starts jogging three mornings a week and feels less overwhelmed. Which lesson strategy is this?"],"compare":"Exercise addresses the body's stress arousal; meditation calms the mind."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-045","term":"Social support buffers stress","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Coping with stress","cue":"people protect against stress","simple":"Social support from friends and family helps buffer the effects of stress.","examples":["The lesson names social support as an effective way to manage stress.","The chapter notes that hostile individuals have less social support and higher stress-related mortality."],"apply":["The quiz says keeping problems to yourself is NOT effective. Which strategy is the opposite?"],"compare":"Isolation raises stress risk; social connectedness lowers it."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-046","term":"Meditation as stress management","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Coping with stress","cue":"meditation calms the mind","simple":"Meditation is an effective technique for managing stress.","examples":["The lesson lists meditation with exercise and social support as effective stress management.","The chapter presents it as a way to respond that reduces a stressor's overall impact."],"apply":["A student uses ten minutes of daily meditation before exams and feels calmer. Which lesson strategy is this?"],"compare":"Problem-focused coping changes the stressor; meditation changes your response to it."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-047","term":"Problem-focused coping is less effective for interpersonal stressors","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Coping with stress","cue":"cannot fix a breakup","simple":"Problem-focused coping works poorly for interpersonal stressors like a romantic breakup.","examples":["The lesson says problem-focused coping is less effective for interpersonal stressors such as a breakup.","The chapter contrasts this with work stressors, where problem-focused coping works well."],"apply":["After a breakup, a student makes lists and plans but still feels awful. What does the lesson say about this approach?"],"compare":"Problem-focused coping fixes the problem; emotion-focused coping soothes the feelings, which fits breakups better."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-048","term":"Emotion-focused coping techniques","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Coping with stress","cue":"soothe the feeling","simple":"Emotion-focused coping techniques include talking to others, leisure activities with friends, and exercise.","examples":["The quiz feedback lists talking to others, enjoying leisure with friends, and exercise as emotion-focused techniques.","The lesson says this strategy targets the negative emotions arising from the situation."],"apply":["Serena eats ice cream after a breakup hoping to feel better. Which coping type is she using?"],"compare":"Problem-focused coping solves the stressor; these techniques manage the emotions it causes."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-049","term":"Hardiness component: commitment","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Personality and stress","cue":"commitment means engage","simple":"Commitment means seeing the world as interesting and seeking involvement rather than withdrawal.","examples":["The lesson defines commitment as seeing the world as interesting and seeking involvement.","The chapter presents commitment as one of three hardiness components that explain resilience."],"apply":["A student joins study groups and campus clubs during a hard semester instead of isolating. Which hardiness component is this?"],"compare":"Commitment is engagement with life; control is belief in influencing events."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-050","term":"Hardiness component: control","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Personality and stress","cue":"control means influence","simple":"In hardiness, control is the belief in your ability to influence events.","examples":["The lesson defines control as one's belief in their ability to influence events.","The chapter links this belief to lower stress under the same circumstances."],"apply":["A student believes her study habits will shape her exam results. Which hardiness component does this show?"],"compare":"Control is about influencing events; challenge is about welcoming change."},{"id":"ch16-lesson-051","term":"Hardiness component: challenge","type":"lesson-concept","category":"Personality and stress","cue":"challenge means growth","simple":"Challenge is seeing change and new experiences as opportunities for learning and growth.","examples":["The lesson defines challenge as seeing change as an opportunity for learning and personal growth.","The chapter lists it with commitment and control as the three parts of hardiness."],"apply":["A student treats transferring schools as an adventure rather than a threat. Which hardiness component is this?"],"compare":"Challenge welcomes change; commitment engages with the world as it is."},{"apply":["A question asks for the leading preventable cause of death in the U.S. Which behavior is the answer?"],"category":"Psychology and health","compare":"Smoking tops the preventable-death list; obesity, alcohol abuse, and inactivity are also risky but rank below it.","cue":"smoking kills most","examples":["The lesson names smoking — not alcohol, inactivity, or obesity — as the top preventable cause of death.","The chapter quiz tests this directly, offering smoking, psychological distress, obesity, and alcohol abuse as options."],"id":"ch16-lesson-052","simple":"Smoking is the leading preventable cause of death in the United States.","term":"Smoking: the leading preventable cause of death","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Someone follows all four habits from the lesson. Roughly how much could their chronic disease risk drop?"],"category":"Psychology and health","compare":"The five behavior domains describe what affects health; the four-habit finding shows how much small daily choices matter.","cue":"four habits, 78% less disease","examples":["The lesson lists the four habits: refraining from smoking, exercising 30 minutes each day, maintaining a healthy weight, and eating a balanced diet rich in fruits and vegetables.","Research cited in the lesson puts the payoff at a 78% reduction in overall chronic disease risk."],"id":"ch16-lesson-053","simple":"Four simple habits — no smoking, 30 minutes of daily exercise, healthy weight, and a fruit-and-vegetable-rich diet — can cut chronic disease risk by 78%.","term":"Four health habits, 78% lower chronic disease risk","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["A community has high need but low use of mental health services and is barely studied. Which disparity does the lesson describe?"],"category":"Psychology and health","compare":"Mental health care disparities are about unequal access and quality of care; health disparities more broadly include unequal rates of illness itself.","cue":"unequal mental health care","examples":["The lesson lists four gaps: less access to services, lower likelihood of receiving them, poorer quality of care, and underrepresentation in mental health research.","Ethnic and cultural differences in health and illness exist both within the United States and across the world."],"id":"ch16-lesson-054","simple":"Underrepresented groups in the U.S. get less access to mental health care, are less likely to receive it, and get poorer quality care.","term":"Mental health care disparities for underrepresented groups","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Why might the same health program work differently in two cultural communities?"],"category":"Psychology and health","compare":"Cultural differences describe how health varies between groups; the biopsychosocial model explains why — biology, psychology, and social factors interact.","cue":"health varies by culture","examples":["The lesson states these differences exist within the U.S. and across the world.","The differences include discrepancies in access to mental health care."],"id":"ch16-lesson-055","simple":"Health and illness differ across ethnic and cultural groups, in the United States and worldwide.","term":"Cultural and ethnic differences in health and illness","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["A student assumes optimists are just born that way. What would Seligman say?"],"category":"Positive psychology","compare":"Learned optimism treats positive thinking as a skill to build; the older view treated it as a trait you either have or don't.","cue":"optimism can be learned","examples":["The lesson credits psychologist Martin Seligman with the idea that optimism is learnable, not fixed.","Research cited in the lesson finds optimism is a significant predictor of positive health outcomes."],"id":"ch16-lesson-056","simple":"Martin Seligman proposed that optimism — thinking positively about the future — can be learned.","term":"Learned optimism (Seligman)","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Two people have similar incomes, but one has close relationships and the other is isolated. Who does the lesson predict is happier?"],"category":"Positive psychology","compare":"Relationships reliably predict happiness; once basic needs are met, extra money does not.","cue":"relationships drive happiness","examples":["The lesson names relationship strength as one critical factor contributing to happiness.","Positive emotions in the lesson include love alongside happiness, gratitude, contentment, and hope."],"id":"ch16-lesson-057","simple":"The strength of your personal relationships is a critical factor in how happy you are.","term":"Personal relationships and happiness","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Joining a club makes someone feel steadier and happier. Which need is being met?"],"category":"Positive psychology","compare":"Belongingness is about group connection; flow is about absorption in an activity.","cue":"need to connect","examples":["The lesson defines belongingness as the need to affiliate with and be part of a group.","It appears alongside flow as a core positive-psychology concept, distinct from gratitude journaling."],"id":"ch16-lesson-058","simple":"Belongingness is the need to connect with and be part of a group.","term":"Belongingness","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Margarite writes one grateful thought each day. Which outcome does the lesson predict?"],"category":"Positive psychology","compare":"Gratitude journaling builds well-being through reflection; flow builds it through immersion.","cue":"write thanks daily","examples":["The lesson's Margarite keeps a gratitude journal, writing daily about something she is grateful for — and it contributes to her well-being.","Gratitude — being thankful and appreciative — is one of the traits the lesson links to well-being, with hope, resilience, and spirituality."],"id":"ch16-lesson-059","simple":"Regularly writing down things you are grateful for builds well-being.","term":"Gratitude journaling and well-being","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["A question asks how positive psychology's focus differs from earlier psychology. What is the key contrast?"],"category":"Positive psychology","compare":"Earlier psychology asked what is wrong; positive psychology asks what is strong — and answers with data.","cue":"strengths, not just disorders","examples":["The lesson contrasts earlier movements' focus on psychological disorders with positive psychology's focus on happiness.","The chapter quiz tests this: positive psychology is scientific, not a softer anecdotal science — it embraces humanistic ideas about growth while using scientific reasoning."],"id":"ch16-lesson-060","simple":"Earlier psychology focused on disorders; positive psychology focuses on strengths, fulfillment, and happiness — using scientific methods.","term":"Positive psychology vs. earlier disorder-focused psychology","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Someone believes the future will be good but has no specific goals. Which trait is that?"],"category":"Positive psychology","compare":"Optimism is a general positive outlook; hope ties that outlook to specific goals.","cue":"optimism vs. hope","examples":["The lesson defines optimism as thinking positively about the future (Seligman) and hope as expecting your goals will be met.","Both are traits the lesson links to well-being, alongside resilience, spirituality, and gratitude."],"id":"ch16-lesson-061","simple":"Optimism is thinking positively about the future; hope is the expectation that your goals will be met.","term":"Optimism vs. hope","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["An artist is so absorbed in painting she forgets to eat, while her friend feels grateful for the art club. Which concept fits each person?"],"category":"Positive psychology","compare":"Flow is absorbed in doing. Belongingness is connected to others. Gratitude is thankful for what is.","cue":"flow, belonging, gratitude","examples":["The lesson's Gerard loses track of time writing — that is flow, not gratitude or belongingness.","The lesson's Margarite writes daily gratitudes — that builds well-being through thankfulness, not through immersion or group connection."],"id":"ch16-lesson-062","simple":"Flow is losing yourself in an activity, belongingness is connecting with a group, and gratitude is appreciating what you have.","term":"Telling flow, belongingness, and gratitude apart","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["A student bounces back from failure with a better plan. Is that resistance or resilience?"],"category":"General adaptation syndrome","compare":"Resistance is a stress-response stage in the body; resilience is a psychological strength of the person.","cue":"resistance is not resilience","examples":["The lesson's GAS resistance phase: the body keeps responding to stress at lower intensity while resources compensate.","The lesson defines resilience as adapting to life's challenges in positive ways — a trait linked to well-being."],"id":"ch16-lesson-063","simple":"Resistance is the body's stage of coping with ongoing stress; resilience is a person's ability to adapt to life's challenges in positive ways.","term":"Resistance vs. resilience","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Years of unrelenting stress end in heart problems. Which term describes stress-caused illness like this?"],"category":"Stress and immune functioning","compare":"Diseases of adaptation come from chronic stress depleting the body; acute stress effects fade once the stressor passes.","cue":"stress-made diseases","examples":["The lesson names cardiovascular problems, allergies, and even life-threatening conditions as diseases of adaptation.","They arise in the exhaustion stage of the general adaptation syndrome, when resources are depleted and the immune system is suppressed."],"id":"ch16-lesson-064","simple":"Diseases of adaptation are health problems caused by prolonged stress wearing the body's resources down.","term":"Diseases of adaptation","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["After the danger passes, heart rate slows and the body settles. Which branch is taking over?"],"category":"Stress and immune functioning","compare":"Sympathetic mobilizes for the threat; parasympathetic recovers after it.","cue":"parasympathetic equals recover","examples":["The lesson's wrap-up: the sympathetic branch mobilizes energy for immediate demands, while the parasympathetic branch promotes recovery and balance.","Stress researchers measure autonomic reactivity — heart rate variability and perspiration — to see these branches at work."],"id":"ch16-lesson-065","simple":"The parasympathetic branch calms the body and restores balance after stress.","term":"Parasympathetic nervous system and recovery","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Someone stays in high gear for months and never truly unwinds. What does the lesson say creates the greatest risk?"],"category":"Stress and immune functioning","compare":"Short-term sympathetic arousal is healthy mobilization; chronic imbalance without recovery is what damages health.","cue":"imbalance harms most","examples":["The lesson's wrap-up states this imbalance — excessive sympathetic activity with insufficient parasympathetic counter-regulation — creates the greatest health risks.","Worldwide, 62% of people say stress has negatively affected their daily life, so this imbalance is common."],"id":"ch16-lesson-066","simple":"The greatest health risk comes from long-term imbalance: too much sympathetic activity with too little parasympathetic recovery.","term":"Autonomic imbalance and long-term health risk","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["A study uses a parent-child conflict task to stress 9- to 12-year-olds, then tests their blood for inflammation. Which study is this?"],"category":"Stress and immune functioning","compare":"The study's design is who and how they tested; its finding was that the autonomic response — not family stress alone — determined inflammation.","cue":"Oshri 2020: harsh parenting","examples":["The lab stressor was a conflict task with each child's primary caregiver.","Researchers measured inflammation through blood tests of C-reactive protein and interleukin-6, and autonomic reactivity through heart rate variability and perspiration."],"id":"ch16-lesson-067","simple":"Oshri and colleagues (2020) studied how family stress — harsh parenting — relates to inflammation in 101 children ages 9 to 12.","term":"Oshri et al. (2020) family stress study","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Which PERMA element do happiness, love, and contentment belong to?"],"category":"Positive psychology","compare":"Positive emotions are one PERMA element; PERMA is the full five-element model of well-being.","cue":"PERMA's P","examples":["The lesson lists these five as the positive emotions in PERMA.","Gratitude journaling (Margarite) is one way the lesson shows positive emotion being cultivated."],"id":"ch16-lesson-068","simple":"Positive emotions — happiness, love, gratitude, contentment, and hope — are the first element of the PERMA model of well-being.","term":"Positive emotions: happiness, love, gratitude, contentment, hope","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Researchers draw blood to check for bodily inflammation. Which two biomarkers from the lesson would they test?"],"category":"Stress and immune functioning","compare":"The biomarkers show whether inflammation is present; the study's question was what drives them up.","cue":"CRP and IL-6","examples":["The Oshri (2020) study measured these two biomarkers through blood tests to detect systemic inflammation.","In the study they served as the dependent variables — the outcomes the researchers tried to explain."],"id":"ch16-lesson-069","simple":"C-reactive protein and interleukin-6 are blood-test biomarkers that rise when there is inflammation in the body.","term":"Inflammation biomarkers: C-reactive protein and interleukin-6","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["A researcher wants to see how strongly a child's nervous system reacts to stress. Which two measures from the lesson fit?"],"category":"Stress and immune functioning","compare":"Heart rate variability and perspiration measure the nervous system's reaction; CRP and interleukin-6 measure the inflammation that may follow.","cue":"HRV and sweat","examples":["The Oshri (2020) study tested autonomic reactivity this way while children did the conflict task.","This reactivity decided whether family stress was linked to higher inflammation — it moderated the effect."],"id":"ch16-lesson-070","simple":"Autonomic nervous system reactivity to stress can be measured through heart rate variability and perspiration.","term":"Measuring autonomic reactivity","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["One researcher studies how stress affects heart disease; another studies what makes people flourish. Which field is each in?"],"category":"Psychology and health","compare":"Health psychology asks how the mind affects the body; positive psychology asks what makes life go well.","cue":"health psych vs positive psych","examples":["The lesson defines health psychology as investigating relationships between psychological variables and health.","The lesson defines positive psychology as identifying and promoting people's strengths and the qualities behind fulfillment and happiness."],"id":"ch16-lesson-071","simple":"Health psychology studies how psychological factors affect health; positive psychology studies strengths and well-being.","term":"Health psychology vs. positive psychology","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Someone cannot remove a chronic stressor but trains in mindfulness. What bodily system from the lesson might improve?"],"category":"Stress and immune functioning","compare":"Most coping targets the stressor or the feelings; mindfulness training can alter the nervous system's stress response itself.","cue":"mindfulness rewires stress","examples":["The lesson's wrap-up states ANS functionality can be altered through behavioral intervention such as mindfulness training.","This matters for prevention: changing the stress response itself, not just avoiding stressors."],"id":"ch16-lesson-072","simple":"Behavioral interventions like mindfulness training can change how the autonomic nervous system handles stress.","term":"Mindfulness training and autonomic regulation","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["A treatment plan addresses genes and stress but ignores social support. What is missing, and why does the model say it matters?"],"category":"Psychology and health","compare":"Biology alone is the medical view; the biopsychosocial view requires all three factors interacting.","cue":"all three, not two","examples":["The lesson's weight example: family history plus personal stresses plus friends' support habits — answers using only two of the three are marked wrong.","Each pair the lesson shows (smoking plus exercise, stresses plus friends, family history plus exercise) misses something the full model catches."],"id":"ch16-lesson-073","simple":"The biopsychosocial model only works when biological, psychological, AND social factors are all considered together.","term":"The biopsychosocial model needs all three factors","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["Someone is strained by bills, a tough boss, and a sick parent. Which stressor domains are these?"],"category":"Stress basics","compare":"Stressor domains are WHERE stress comes from; brief versus chronic describes HOW LONG it lasts.","cue":"money, work, health, family","examples":["The lesson's research intro lists these five domains as stresses that can affect people.","Worldwide, 62% of people say stress has negatively affected their daily life — these ordinary domains are why."],"id":"ch16-lesson-074","simple":"The stressors that wear people down cluster in everyday domains: financial, workplace, health, family, and social.","term":"Everyday stressor domains","type":"lesson-concept"},{"apply":["One student joins a team and thrives; another is isolated and sleeps poorly. Which concept fits each?"],"category":"Positive psychology","compare":"Belongingness is the need; loneliness is the cost of the need going unmet.","cue":"need vs. lacking it","examples":["The lesson defines belongingness as the need to affiliate with and be part of a group.","The chapter quiz shows loneliness harms sleep, raises stress, and increases depression risk — affecting physical and mental health."],"id":"ch16-lesson-075","simple":"Belongingness is the need to connect with a group; loneliness is the health risk of going without that connection.","term":"Belongingness vs. loneliness","type":"lesson-concept"}];